A||||8||Alex4mula||Please help on possible tranny vibration||||1||
Z||000000||Alex4mula||09-10-2001||11:42 AM||||This is my first auto Formula so I'm clueless. When I bought the car I felt this vibration in the back only on hard acceleration events. It was more noticeable with a passenger (added weight?) and accelerating while cornering. I changed the tranny fluid and filter and replaced a broken tranny mount. After this it did better but still could feel it a little.<BR>Well... yesterday I went for his first long trip aftet I bought it. I drove 60 miles each way. When returning I was doing 75 to 80 MPH most of the time. These auto cars run near 3500 RPM at that speed (hate that). I was using the cruise and after about 45 minutes the same vibration started when the cruise accelerated. I disengaged the cruise and if I tried to accelerate over 1/4 throttle the car started to shake badly. If I just partially accelerate it did not. I stopped and checked everything back there including the tranny fluid level. Everything was ok. After about 10 minutes I went on and it didn't do it again, at least as hard as it did before. I had check before the bearings, suspension, etc. and everything is tight back there. I suspect that the tranny oil heated too much and maybe that caused some slippage or something. Is that possible? Any other suggestion on this problem? Is my tranny going bad? Thanks.||||reg||1
Z||000001||starfighter007@msn.com||09-10-2001||11:47 AM||||I would check to see that all your motor mounts are good including the dog bone.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Alex4mula||09-10-2001||01:01 PM||||They are OK. The dogbone is a little bit sloppy but then if that was the case why is this intermittent?||||reg||1
Z||000003||Alex4mula||09-10-2001||04:59 PM||||??||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||09-10-2001||06:52 PM||||A sloppy dodbone can allow intermitant oscilation of the drivetran.<P>In this case it sounds more like TC lockup shudder. Disconnect the electrical harness on the front of the tranny and see if it goes away. Make sure you tie the connector away from things that can eat/melt it.<P>If that stops it,<BR>The TC valve could be crappy... common problem. Cheap to fix. just replace the valve. (I'd do that anyway just to eliminate the posibility.)<BR>The TC itself could be flaky... happens.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 09-10-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||Standard||09-10-2001||11:09 PM||||Could also be a CV joint.  After the boot came off my inner CV joint and spewed grease all over it would shudder on hard acceleration.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Alex4mula||09-11-2001||08:11 AM||||Thanks a lot. I'll replace the TC valve and will inspect the CV joints more thoroughly. Will reoprt after that.<P>How much this valve cost? How hard is to replace it? Thanks. <p>[This message has been edited by Alex4mula (edited 09-11-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||Alex4mula||09-11-2001||01:15 PM||||*bump*||||reg||1
Z||000008||theogre||09-11-2001||08:01 PM||||If it's a TC issue, unplugging it will tell you. That requires no tools.<P>I ment if that does solve it... replace the TC solinoid in the tranny first... the solinoid is known for problems, and it allot easier to replace that then pull a tranny. Even if you did end up pulling the tranny you'd sill want to replace the valve...||||reg||1
